# KNOWN GAP — `todo/t1527`, THE MIRROR DIRECTION. `gg check` ACCEPTS this
# program and it prints a raw heap pointer instead of `8`.
#
# ⛔ THIS IS NOT THE SAME DEFECT AS ITS SIBLING REPRO, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
# WHOLE REASON IT EXISTS. `known_gaps/shared_var_coerced_to_wrapper_param_segv.gg`
# is `unify` arm A — a bare scalar handed to a parameter whose ABI wants a
# pointer, which SIGSEGVs. This is arm B, the exact inverse: a real
# `Mutex[int]` HANDLE handed to a parameter that wants an `int64_t`, so the
# pointer is read as an integer. Same coercion rule, opposite direction,
# and a different failure mode:
#
#   arm A   `-Wint-conversion` (int64_t -> void*)   SIGSEGV, signal 11
#   arm B   `-Wint-conversion` (void* -> int64_t)   rc 0, SILENT WRONG OUTPUT
#
# MEASURED at `24156ed68`: `gg check` rc 0; C emits `warning: passing argument 1
# of 'take_int' makes integer from pointer without a cast [-Wint-conversion]`
# ("expected 'int64_t' but argument is of type 'void *'"); the program exits 0
# having printed `187651999621793`. ⚠ An exit-code gate cannot see this one at
# all — it is the quieter and, by the round's own severity ranking,
# silent-wrong-output rather than a crash.
#
# ⚖ INTENDED — AND THE DIRECTION IS AN OWNER ASK (`todo/t1527`), WHICH THIS
# FIXTURE'S EXISTENCE SHARPENS. This asserts the ACCEPT direction: `8`.
# ⛔ But note what "make it work" MEANS here, because it is not what it means
# for arm A. Arm A would have to MATERIALIZE a wrapper around a scalar. Arm B
# would have to UNWRAP a live `Mutex[int]` into an `int` at an argument
# boundary — an IMPLICIT LOCK ACQUISITION, which D53's lock discipline exists to
# make the author write. So the two directions do not share an answer, and the
# ask has to be put as two questions rather than one.
#
# Asserted by `known_gap_mutex_coerced_to_int_arg_wrong_output`.

int take_int(int x):
    return x + 1

void main():
    Mutex[int] m = Mutex[int](7)
    print(take_int(m))
